plato-phaedo-1.md:1: argue/readings: S2 is read 2 ways: plato-phaedo-1.logic.r1 and plato-phaedo-1.logic.r1.2 have "fearless ∧ noble_bearing" and "fearless ∧ noble_bearing → appeared_blessed" and "appeared_blessed → no_pity"; plato-phaedo-1.logic.r1.3 has "fearless" and "noble_bearing" and "fearless ∧ noble_bearing → appeared_blessed"; they differ when only "Socrates died fearlessly", "his words and bearing were noble and gracious" and "to Phaedo he appeared blessed" are true, which the second allows [in 1 of 2 runs]
plato-phaedo-1.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered no, which keeps plato-phaedo-1.logic.r1 and plato-phaedo-1.logic.r1.2, to: Suppose "Socrates died fearlessly", "his words and bearing were noble and gracious" and "to Phaedo he appeared blessed" are true and "Phaedo did not pity him" is false. Is S2, "For I could hardly believe that I was present at the death of a friend, and therefore I did not pity him, Echecrates; he died so fearlessly, and his words and bearing were so noble and gracious, that to me he appeared blessed.", true then? [in 1 of 2 runs]
plato-phaedo-1.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: plato-phaedo-1.logic.r1 and plato-phaedo-1.logic.r1.2 have "divine_call ∧ happy_there" and "divine_call ∧ happy_there → no_pity"; plato-phaedo-1.logic.r1.3 has "divine_call" and "happy_there"; they differ when only "in going to the other world he could not be without a divine call" and "he would be happy, if any man ever was, when he arrived there" are true, which the second allows [in 1 of 2 runs]
plato-phaedo-1.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered no, which keeps plato-phaedo-1.logic.r1 and plato-phaedo-1.logic.r1.2, to: Suppose "in going to the other world he could not be without a divine call" and "he would be happy, if any man ever was, when he arrived there" are true and "Phaedo did not pity him" is false. Is S3, "I thought that in going to the other world he could not be without a divine call, and that he would be happy, if any man ever was, when he arrived there, and therefore I did not pity him as might have seemed natural at such an hour.", true then? [in 1 of 2 runs]
plato-phaedo-1.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: plato-phaedo-1.logic.r1 and plato-phaedo-1.logic.r1.2 have nothing from it; plato-phaedo-1.logic.r1.3 has "appeared_blessed → no_pity" and "divine_call ∧ happy_there → no_pity"; they differ when only "to Phaedo he appeared blessed", "in going to the other world he could not be without a divine call" and "he would be happy, if any man ever was, when he arrived there" are true, which the first allows [in 1 of 2 runs]
